[{"role": "assistant", "content": "Parallelizing this process can get tricky, especially because adding values to the same variable simultaneously from different threads can yield unexpected results due to race conditions. .NET Framework provides certain types of collections that can be securely used from multiple threads, such as the ConcurrentBag class.

Moreover, .NET Framework provides the Parallel class which has the For and ForEach methods that support parallel implementation of a loop. We can utilize Parallel.ForEach and an instance of ConcurrentBag to handle this in a thread-safe manner.

Here is the sample code in C#:

```cpp
using System;
using System.Collections.Concurrent;
using System.Threading.Tasks;

namespace ConsoleApp
{
    class Program
    {
        static void Main(string[] args)
        {
            int[] array = { 7, 14, 23, 27, 33 };
            ConcurrentBag<int> bag = new ConcurrentBag<int>();

            // Store each value into the thread-safe bag
            Parallel.ForEach(array, value => {
                bag.Add(value);
            });

            // Use a single thread to calculate the sum to avoid race conditions
            int sum = 0;
            foreach (var value in bag)
            {
                sum += value;
            }

            Console.WriteLine("Sum = " + sum); // this should output "Sum = 104"
            Console.ReadLine();
        }
    }
}
```

This code safely performs parallel processing by adding each value from the array into a ConcurrentBag. Then, it adds up all values stored in the ConcurrentBag using a single thread.

Please note that this is indeed not a very efficient solution for small sized array as the overhead of thread management takes more time than simple sequential computation. However, for significantly large sized arrays, this parallel computation would trump over sequential computation."}]

