This program calculates the Nth Fibonacci number using recursion and memoization, which is a common technique for optimizing recursive algorithms when the same results are calculated many times.

```cpp
#include<iostream>
using namespace std;

const int MAX = 100;
long long fiboMemo[MAX] = {0};

long long fibonacci(int N) {
  if (N == 0) return 0;
  if (N == 1) return 1;
  if (fiboMemo[N] != 0) return fiboMemo[N]; // Avoid re-calculation
  fiboMemo[N] = fibonacci(N - 1) + fibonacci(N - 2); // Memoize
  return fiboMemo[N];
}

int main () {
  int N;
  cout << "Enter a number: ";
  cin >> N;
  cout << "Fibonacci #" << N << " = " << fibonacci(N) << endl;
  return 0;
}
```
In this version, the array `fiboMemo` stores previously computed Fibonacci numbers as soon as they are calculated. Thus, this version avoids the redundant computation present in the pure recursive version.

Note that due to the nature of the Fibonacci sequence (each number is the sum of the previous two), the actual values grow extremely quickly (exponentially). Therefore, even for relatively small values of `N`, the result will be quite large and can easily exceed the limit of the `int` or even `long long` data type in C++. In this program. To see the correct answer for larger values of `N`, you would need some sort of "bignum" handling that allows for arbitrary-precision arithmetic."}]

