#include<iostream>
#include<map>
#include<thread>
#include<mutex>

std::map<char, int> characterCounts;
std::mutex mtx; 

void countCharacters(std::string str) {
    std::map<char, int> localCounts;
    for (char& c : str) {
        if(localCounts.find(c) != localCounts.end()) {
            localCounts[c]++;
        } else {
            localCounts[c] = 1;
        }
    }

    // Lock the mutex before changing the shared resource
    mtx.lock();
    for(auto& count : localCounts){
        if(characterCounts.find(count.first) != characterCounts.end()){
            characterCounts[count.first] += count.second;
        } else {
            characterCounts[count.first] = count.second;
        }
    }
    // Unlock the resource when done
    mtx.unlock();
}

int main() {
    std::thread t1(countCharacters, "Hello, World!");
    std::thread t2(countCharacters, "Multithreading in C++!");

    t1.join();
    t2.join();

    for(auto kv : characterCounts) {
        std::cout << kv.first << ": " << kv.second << std::endl;
    }

    return 0;
}
```
In this example, each thread calculates its own local character counts, so no synchronization is needed when reading the input strings. After that, a lock is acquired to safely add local counts to the global `characterCounts` map - this is where the data race would occur. After the addition, the lock is released, allowing other threads to add their counts.

The benefit of this approach is that most of the work, which is counting characters in individual strings, is done concurrently without needing synchronization. Only when writing results to the shared data structure is synchronization needed. This design could significantly improve performance if counting characters is the primary operation."}]

