[{"role": "assistant", "content": "Here is the correct recursive implementation of Bubble Sort in C++:

```cpp
#include <iostream>

void bubbleSort(int arr[], int n) {
    if (n == 1) {
        return;
    }

    for (int i = 0; i < n-1; i++) {
        if (arr[i] > arr[i+1]) {
            std::swap(arr[i], arr[i+1]);
        }
    }

    bubbleSort(arr, n-1);
}

int main() {
    int arr[10000];  // Assuming array size of up to 10,000 elements

    // Populate the array with random integers
    for (int i = 0; i < 10000; i++) {
        arr[i] = rand() % 10000;
    }

    int n = sizeof(arr) / sizeof(arr[0]);

    bubbleSort(arr, n);

    // Print the sorted array
    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        std::cout << arr[i] << " ";
    }

    return 0;
}
```

The above code initializes an array of size 10,000 and populates it with random integers. It then calculates the size of the array and calls the `bubbleSort` function to sort the array recursively. Finally, it prints the sorted array.

The `bubbleSort` function checks if the array size is 1, in which case it returns as the array is already sorted. Otherwise, it performs a single pass of the bubble sort algorithm by comparing adjacent elements and swapping them if they are in the wrong order. Then, it recursively calls the `bubbleSort` function with a smaller array size (n-1).

This implementation has a time complexity of O(n^2) and does not use any extra space other than the given array."}]

